How to Build a Balanced Period Plate

Eating well during your period does not require a complicated meal plan. One simple approach is to build meals using several food groups, including vegetables, fruits, carbohydrates, protein, and healthy fats.

For lunch, you could have rice, dal, mixed vegetables, and curd. This provides carbohydrates, protein, fibre, and several vitamins and minerals.

If you prefer chapati, try chapati with chickpea curry, leafy greens, and a salad. You can add yoghurt if you enjoy dairy.

Breakfast can also be balanced. Oatmeal with banana, nuts, and yoghurt is one example. Another option is eggs with whole-grain toast, vegetables, and fruit.

Snacks can include combinations rather than only processed foods. Fruit with nuts, yoghurt with seeds, roasted chickpeas, or banana with peanut butter can all be simple choices.

Iron-containing foods are worth including regularly. Lentils, beans, chickpeas, leafy greens, tofu, eggs, and meat can contribute iron. Plant-based iron sources can be paired with vitamin C-rich foods such as tomatoes, citrus fruits, and guava.

Healthy fats can come from nuts, seeds, peanuts, avocado, and suitable cooking oils. You do not need to remove fats from your diet.

Carbohydrates are also important because they provide energy. Rice, oats, potatoes, whole grains, bread, and other carbohydrate-containing foods can all fit into a balanced diet.

Hydration should be included in your routine too. Water is the easiest choice, while soups and water-rich fruits can contribute additional fluids.

There is no need to completely remove sweets, fried foods, or your favourite snacks. Enjoying these foods occasionally can fit into an overall balanced eating pattern.

Food cannot guarantee that period pain or other symptoms will disappear. If your periods are unusually painful, extremely heavy, or regularly interfere with your daily activities, speaking with a healthcare professional is important.

Menstrual hygiene is another part of creating a comfortable period routine. Choose sanitary pads according to your flow and personal preferences, and change them regularly.

If you are heading out, carry an extra pad in your handbag or backpack. Being prepared can help reduce stress if your period lasts longer or your flow changes.

